%[text] %[text:anchor:T_82129B4F] # Sharing Code
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:MainMenu.m)
%[text] Writing code for yourself is the first step, but when you create something useful or interesting, you also need to know how to share your work with others. For a few lines of code or a simple script, you could use a chat system or email. Sometimes you will want to know what has changed between one version of a file and another. If the script is short, you may be able to look at them side-by-side to see the differences. As your code gets more complex, this quickly becomes impractical. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A screenshot of code being compared using the Code Compare tool. Orange areas with code on the left pair with grey blanks on the right; grey blanks on the left pair with blue code on the right; and purple areas align on both left and right with differences highlighted in darker purple. Content which is identical in both versions is light grey.](text:image:81f5)
%[text] This script offers a brief introduction to sharing your code with others.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0EF068D3] ## Projects
%[text] You can gather MATLAB code into [projects](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/matlab_prog/create-projects.html) that give the creator control over adding a set of scripts to the MATLAB path (and removing them properly when a project is closed) and the ability to run code when a project is [loaded](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/matlab_prog/manage-project-settings-path-labels-and-startup-and-shutdown-tasks.html) or unloaded. 
%[text] ![](text:image:5352) **Try**.
%[text]     1\. Select the **Project** tab in the Live Editor. This will give you a different set of Toolstrip tabs:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:1ec6)
%[text] Select **Project Issues** to open the Project Issues panel.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:26fe)
%[text] In the Project Issues panel, select ![Run checks button](text:image:3fa1). Are there any new files that you have created that are not currently part of the project?
%[text]     2\. Observe the contents of the Project panel:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Screenshot of the project panel for Programming: Structuring Code](text:image:940b)
%[text]     3\. In the **Files** panel, right-click on the project file `StructuringCode.prj` and select **Create Zip file**. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:6e48)
%[text] This will package up all the components of your project as a single file. You can then easily share the entire project as a single file with any other MATLAB user. 
%%
%[text] ## Comparing Code
%[text] A diff between two versions of a file is a record of all of the things that have changed between the two files. Identifying these differences is straightforward if the files are purely text, e.g., .m files, or if they are images of the same size. However, it is more complicated to implement with binary files or other forms of rich text such as .mlx files. 
%[text] ![](text:image:936d) **Try**. 
%[text] 1. Use the ![compare code icon](text:image:976e) **Compare** button in the **Live Editor** tab on the toolstrip to compare `showTaylor.m` and `showTaylor2.m`, which are both in the H`elperFunctions` file. The Comparison Type should be "Live code comparison".
%[text] 2. What differences do you find? Is there a bug in either file?
%[text] 3. If necessary, use ![Swap Sides icon](text:image:1554)**Swap Sides** to ensure that `showTaylor.m` is the file on the left and `showTaylor2.m` is the file on the right. Then select ![Merge Text icon](text:image:9850) **Merge Mode**`.`
%[text] 4. In the column between the files, for each line or block of code that differs, there will be a ![](text:image:48de) **Replace Content** icon. Scroll through the comparison and select one or more lines to be replaced. What happens?
%[text] 5. Save your changes and observe the results. \
%[text] ![](text:image:7794) **Reflect**.
%[text] 1. When will you want to use this type of comparison if you are working alone? What if you are working with other people? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6529D573] ## Version Control
%[text] Version control preserves the state of a document at various points in time. There is nothing extraordinary about version control for code over any other system that allows multiple authors to collaborate on a document and observe each other's changes. Version control systems for code incorporate branching/forking, pulls/pushes, and merging. MATLAB supports the use of two version control systems by default: Git and SVN. 
%[text] ### Concepts and Vocabulary
%[text] This is intended as a glossary of terms that you are likely to encounter as you begin to work with Git, but you may not encounter them all at once.
%[text] **Source control**, also known as version control, is a system that manages changes to documents, computer programs, and other collections of information. Any time there are earlier versions of a document saved that you can return to, that is an example of source control. It is especially useful in collaborative environments where multiple people are working on the same project. Imagine writing a group paper where everyone can work on their sections simultaneously. Source control keeps track of each person's edits, allowing the team to see who made changes and when. It also enables you to revert to previous versions if needed, much like an advanced "undo" button. This system helps prevent conflicts and data loss, ensuring that everyone is working with the most up-to-date information. One popular tool for source control is Git, which is widely used in software development but can be applied to any project involving digital files.
%[text] **Git:** A common tool used for source control.
%[text] **repository:** A folder containing files and the Git history of those files.
%[text] **license:** The legal agreement lays out who can use the content in a Git repository and how they are allowed to use it. This repository is shared under a [BSD-3 license](file:LICENSE.md).
%[text] **remote:** A shorthand way to refer to the cloud location where you are storing the shared copy of your repository. In this case, you will use a remote repository on GitHub. 
%[text] **clone:** The Git term for creating the initial local copy of a Git repository.
%[text] **pull:** The action of updating your local copy of a repository to match the version stored in the remote repository using Git. 
%[text] **commit:** The action of creating a saved version of your files in a Git repository. You will also be prompted to add a **commit message** where you describe the changes you have made from the previous saved version.
%[text] **branch:** The term Git uses to describe different, but related, versions of your folder and the files it contains that are being developed in parallel. In this case, you will only use the main branch and will not create any other branches. 
%[text] **push**: The action of using your local repository to update the version in a remote location, such as GitHub, using Git.
%[text] **SHA**: A hexadecimal (alphanumeric) string that identifies each commit for Git, usually displayed as the last seven digits: e.g. d9ef0e5.
%%
%[text] ## Creating a Git Repository
%[text] ### Locally, in your current directory
%[text] If you are working in MATLAB and would like to use Git as source control, you can turn your current directory into a local Git repository by running the command:
gitinit
%[text] Note that this command will error if the current directory is already a Git repository.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_94AE8854] ### From a remote repository by cloning
%[text] Making your own copy of a Git repository is called cloning. Cloning copies the current version of the files, and the change history from the repository, into a location you control. It will also preserve a link to the repository you cloned so you can offer changes you make locally back to the original repository. Step-by-step instructions are given in the documentation: [Clone a Git Repository](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/matlab_prog/clone-git-repository.html). In this script, we will not explore the landscape of pushing changes upstream or making pull requests. We will make a local copy of an interesting repository to play with. 
%[text] ![](text:image:51b9) **Try**.
%[text] 1. Locate the address of the repository that you want to clone. In GitHub, this looks like: \
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Select the Code button, and locate the https address for the repository](text:image:373e)
%[text] For the "How to Turn Your Script into a Simple App" repository, the address is: 
%[text] [https://github.com/mathworks/how-to-turn-your-script-into-a-simple-app.git](https://github.com/mathworks/how-to-turn-your-script-into-a-simple-app)
%[text] ![MATLAB Icon](text:image:5c86) **Pro-tip**. If you are going to work with the remote system regularly, it may be worth setting up an SSH key, but for now you can use the HTTPS address to clone this public repository. Use the button with overlapping squares to copy the address to your clipboard.
%[text]           2\. In the **Home** tab of the toolstrip, select **New**. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:6b44)
%[text] In that dropdown menu, **Git Clone**. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:19e0)
%[text] Paste the address of the repository you want to clone into the **URL** field:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Screenshot of Clone Git Repository popup showing Clone from: URL with URL https://github.com/mathworks/how-to-turn-your-script-into-a-simple-app.git and folder C:\Users\Default\Documents\how-to-turn-your-script-into-a-simple-app. The checkbox is unselected and the buttons are "Clone" and "Cancel"](text:image:3b84)
%[text] If you want, you can change the Folder. Then select **Clone**. The folder chosen must be empty.
%[text]           3\. Go explore!
%[text] ![Warning Symbol](text:image:7adf) Ensure that any new Git repository contains a `.gitattributes` file that identifies any MATLAB or Simulink file extensions (e.g., .mlx, .mat, .fig, .mdl, .slx, etc) as binary formats. If you use Git through MATLAB, this will happen automatically, but if you use an external program, it may not. See the [documentation](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/matlab_prog/set-up-git-source-control.html) for more details, including a command to create a `.gitattributes` file containing the standard MATLAB and Simulink file formats.  
%%
%[text] ## Standard Git Workflow
%[text] ### Committing to a Git Repository 
%[text] The process of telling Git that you want to save the current version for future reference is called "committing" and the description of the current state and/or changes that have been made from the previous committed version is called a "commit message".
%[text] First, ensure that you have defined appropriate credentials in MATLAB Settings ![Setting icon](text:image:3d01) under Source Control:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:6b0c)
%[text] You can use the Source Control panel ![](text:image:4fbc) to identify and commit changes to your files.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:0f82)
%[text] Clicking the Commit button will open up a Commit dialog like this one:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:2795)
%[text] Enter the commit message in the box on the right, here it is "Add an updated screenshot of creating a Git repository to SharingCode", then click Commit. You will not be able to commit without writing a commit message as it is part of the required structure for Git.
%%
%[text] ### Pulling From a Remote Repository
%[text] Pulling from a remote repository updates the state of your local repository with any changes that have been made to the remote one. If you are the only person working on a project, this is not likely to happen a lot but you may still make changes, such as edits to the README, in the remote version. If you are working with other people, this is a great way to share code and updates. 
%[text] In a repository that you have cloned, you will immediately be able to pull from the Source Control section
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:0e91)
%[text] Or within the Source Control panel:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:540c)
%[text] ### Pushing To a Remote Repository
%[text] If you have appropriate permissions to add your work to a remote repository, you can also use the Source Control section of the Project toolstrip to **Push** which will update the remote repository with the local commits that you have made. It is always best practice, even when working alone, to **Pull** immediately before you **Push**, just to make sure you know what you are trying to add to. 
%[text] ### Working With Collaborators: Branching and Merging
%[text] To keep a clean, common copy of a joint project, individual collaborators will usually create their own branch to work on and periodically merge work that they wish to share back into the main branch. Merging is the process of choosing which pieces of conflicting work generated by different contributors will be combined to create the saved copy. Detailed instructions can be found in the documentation for [branches](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/matlab_prog/create-manage-and-merge-git-branches.html) and [merging](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/matlab_prog/resolve-git-conflicts-in-matlab.html). 
%%
